# arvo-hash, backlog

> **Type-surface reminder**: new entries use `arvo::USize` /
> `Bool` / `UFixed<N, 0, S>` instead of `usize` / `bool` / `u*`;
> `notko::Maybe` / `Outcome` instead of `Option` / `Result`. See
> `rules/type-surface.md`.

## Concrete algorithms

Shipping order as consumers surface need:

- Fnv1a: shipped (, refactored to bounded-generic impl). Generic algorithm `Fnv1a<const N: u16>` impls `Hasher<N>` once and `ConstHash<N, Hot, Unsigned>` once, replacing the prior 64-impl macro paste.
- XxHash3: shipped as arvo-hash's default hash family; same bounded-generic shape after.
- XxHash64: when a faster non-cryptographic hash is wanted (separate algorithm-state width from XxHash3).
- Crc32: when checksum use cases surface (clause bytecode integrity, persistence file checksumming).

Each future concrete algorithm lands as a struct implementing `Hasher<const N: u16>` (streaming) and `ConstHash<N, Hot, Unsigned>` (one-shot const-callable). Non-Hot strategy `ConstHash` impls land per-consumer-need.

## Fnv1a128 algorithm

`Fnv1a<const N: u16>` produces at most 64 bits of state (FNV-1a-64 algorithm with low-N masking). For widths beyond 64, a separate Fnv1a128 algorithm with 128-bit state is required. Lands when a consumer surfaces a need for >64-bit non-cryptographic hashes.

## Hilavitkutin-str migration

hilavitkutin-str const_fnv1a is the existing FNV implementation.
Once Fnv1a28 lands in arvo-hash, migrate hilavitkutin-str to
import or re-export that algo and adjust downstream use sites.

## Wider bit widths

If arvo-bits grows u128 backing for Bits (its own BACKLOG item),
arvo-hash gains Hash128-domain semantic aliases.

## Cryptographic hashes

Out of scope. arvo-hash targets non-cryptographic identity hashes.
A sibling arvo-crypto crate may appear if cryptographic hash
needs surface.

## Semantic aliases

NameHash, KeyHash, or similar named aliases beyond ContentHash
land once consumer patterns stabilise. The starting form is a
local alias in the consuming crate over ContentHash or Bits<N>;
promotion happens when a second consumer shares the alias.
